o that's one  possibility  so you know ideally formulation is  flexible enough to accommodate a whole  range of things you know that may or may  not include therapy you've mentioned  throughout the interview certain words  that you don't really want to use that  are associated with them with mental  health or our part of our vocabulary at  the moment and surrounding mental health  and I don't like the word mental health  either  really  okay so and so maybe you could run us  through a series of terms that you think  are wrong and propose alternatives for  each of them I mean the reason I'm a bit  hung up on language is because we use  the language of medical thinking so  uncritically in what what I wouldn't  call mental health but it is officially  called a mental health system so if you  use a word like illness or disorder or  symptom location or treatment or cure or  prognosis or you know there's a whole  range of words all of those actually  imply that we're dealing with medical  illnesses and unless you're quite  careful about language I think that  assumption kind of creeps in it creeps  in we really need to change your  thinking in this area because the  medical approach is so deeply embedded  in our whole way of thinking and that's  not just mental health professionals  it's everybody what do you read in the  media everybody thinks there's such a  thing as a medical illness called  psychosis or schizophrenia or clinical  depression you know there isn't  particularly thinks there is and one of  the most effective ways of challenging  that is to be very careful about your  language change the way you talk you are  forced to challenge some of your  assumptions so what do you do instead  and this is a very controversial issue  by the way lots of people don't like  language being challenged but I mean  everyone can choose that arm language of  course they can but I think it has to be  an open choice and an informal choice  and if we're all automatically using  medical language in effect we're  depriving people of other choices so my  argument and the argument of many other  people who I work with would be to use  the most ordinary everyday neutral  language we can so I will use ordinary  words like extreme distress or emotional  suffering or extreme states or problems  or difficulties or struggles or recovery  or healing and there's not a single set  of words to replace the ones we have but  there's a you know there's ordinary  language words which we all understand  which don't imply medic  and you also use the word story a lot we  talk about that a bit about that why is  it important to have a kind of coherent  story that about one's life okay so if  you asked me in very simply as people  sometimes do what's the alternative to  diagnosis I would say it's listening to  people's stories so stories one word  narrative is another word formulation is  a particular kind of story but in  essence I think what's most damaging  about diagnosis is that it skews  people's personal stories you know  everybody comes along to mental health  services or experiences distress without  coming over to services for reasons in  their lives everybody has a story and if  you impose a diagnosis in effect you're  shutting down that story you're saying  it's because you've got whatever it is  bipolar disorder but it's now disorder  clinical depression as soon as you say  that you tend to stop asking and you've  got this false explanation which sounds  like an explanation it isn't why are you  feeling so MS real desperate because  you've got clinical depression how do we  know you've got clinical depression  because you're feeling miserable and  desperate why are you having the  terrible mood swings because you have  bipolar disorder how do they've got my  purchase order because you're having  these terrible mood swings and  underneath all those labels there will  be stories and I have a particular  interest in formulation but I'm more  generally uninterested in and supportive  of any type of storytelling any type of  narrative I think formulation works  quite well in services because it's got  some credibility and some research to  support it but in general if it makes  sense to you to think about your story  through art or through poetry or through  music or through you know community  rituals or myths and legends many of  which still exist as a way of making  sense of distress in parts of the world  that haven't yet been colonized by our  diagnostic model you know all of that is  great it's kind of what works and make  sense for you really I'm I'm speaking of  other cultures and have you looked at  the way  other cultures deal with distress and  what we could learn yeah I have to an  extent and part of have done that is  through a project I've been involved in  which I could mention if that's  appropriate so for the last five or six  years I've been involved in a very major  project that is attempting to outline  what would a non diagnostic  understanding of distress looks like  because although we have a number of  examples of what you might do  differently one to one what we're  lacking I think I have been lacking is a  really coherent whole conceptual model  whole way of thinking that could  actually replace diagnostic medical  thinking so there's a core group of nine  of us psychologists and people who call  them survivors at the psychiatric system  together with a number of others who  were funded by the division of clinical  psychology which is my professional body  to undertake this crazily ambitious  project which ended up 200 thousand  words long but there are shorter and  more accessible versions and in essence  it's called the power threat meaning  framework so if you google power threat  meaning framework and British  Psychological Society you'll find all  the resources the documents videos short  versions whatever else you might be  interested in so in a way the essence of  that is establishing 